; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 1.79s\n      1 test result: ok. 29 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 1.20s\n      1 test result: ok. 29 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 1.32s\n      1 test result: ok. 29 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 28.66s\n      1 test result: ok. 29 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 28.85s","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_d45aptkybz7qzrav2ucfoopwymmwezymnflyehlpqbxsww57ak7a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four build configurations pass: 4× symbol parity, 4× 29 Phase B tests, 4× 17 Phase C tests.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe library is tiny (one C function, `bitwriter_add`, 24 lines) but the verification found **two real translation bugs**.\n\n**Phase A** — `Cargo.toml` had no `[features]` and `CMakeLists.txt` has no `option()`/`#ifdef`, so there is exactly **one** feature combination. I verified it under both `--no-default-features` and default, × `dev`/`release` = 4 configurations. Artifacts: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (15 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (29 rows), plus `VERIFICATION.md`.\n\n**Bugs found and fixed.** The arithmetic translation was already correct — I confirmed it statement-by-statement against the `-O0` x86-64 disassembly (32-bit wrapping `add`/`sub`, `shlq/shrq %cl` masking shift counts to 6 bits). The bugs were both in pointer handling, caused by the original `let bw = unsafe { &mut *bw };`:\n\n| input | C | Rust (before) | fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| misaligned `bw` | returns `0`, updates state | `misaligned pointer dereference` → **SIGABRT** | `addr_of_mut!` + `read_unaligned`/`write_unaligned`, never forming a reference |\n| `bw == NULL` | **SIGSEGV** (11) | Rust null check → **SIGABRT** (6) | same fix — the first touch is `bw->tot` at offset 20, so the fault address `0x14` is non-null and a genuine SIGSEGV is raised |\n\nBoth only appeared in the `dev` profile (Rust's UB checks are compiled out in `release`), so a release-only run would have missed them.\n\n**Two process problems worth flagging:**\n- `cargo test` does **not** rebuild the `cdylib`, so the suite silently tested a *stale* `.so` once — the misalignment fix appeared not to work. `tests/common/mod.rs` now refuses to run if either `.so` is older than its source, and `run_all.sh` always builds first.\n- Rather than trusting the suite, I ran a mutation-based negative control: **25/25 injected bugs caught**. Three mutations were *not* caught; instead of assuming a blind spot, I proved by brute force (1.3M structured + 40M random pairs) that the loop makes **at most one progressing iteration**, so spins 2–100 are idempotent. Hence every cap ≥ 1 is observationally identical to `i < 100` (confirmed by an 8M-case sweep: caps 1/2/3/99/101/1000 all agree; cap 0 differs in 41% of cases and *is* caught), and `b > bits` ≡ `b >= bits` since the arms differ only at the tie. These are genuine **equivalent mutants**, now documented with proofs — and that analysis surfaced 3 additional reachable code paths I'd missed (CONFIGS rows 27–29, including the near-2³² `bw->bits` band where `63 - bw->bits` wraps to a *small* positive value).\n\nI did not modify anything in `c_src/` (only added the `build/` directory the task's own instructions call for)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1010,"cache_read_input_tokens":154006,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1010,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"35dbed96-b4a4-4d8d-8740-1664d0d95d52","uuid":"97c5446b-7e94-4ff7-a1c4-573c7bc60e01","timestamp":"2026-08-19T11:32:42.814Z"}
